\subsection{Comparison with ALFALFA Spectra}
\label{sec:spectra}
\noindent
%As in the case of the integrated flux map, we want to create spectra using a method which only integrates where actual emission exists. However, because the intensities in the variable-mask image cube are strictly positive, a spectrum produced from it will have highly non-Gaussian, misleading noise properties. To this end, we use the integrated flux map to produce another mask. As with masks made from the image cube, the integrated flux map is smoothed with a Gaussian twice the size of the restoring beam, then clipped at the 3$\sigma$ level to produce a mask. This one mask is then applied to every channel of the original, unmasked image cube. We refer to this cube as the `spectrum cube,' and produce spectra by integrating the emission over the each channel uniformly.
As global spectra and spectra-derived quantities are the primary data products of ALFALFA, we use these to evaluate the quality of our VLA observations, determine whether significant flux was missed, and double-check our data reduction approach. Figure \ref{fig:spectra} shows our global VLA spectra for UGC 9037 and UGC 12506. The spectra were produced by integrating the data cubes where the flux map indicates that significant ($>3\sigma$) emission exists. The single-dish ALFALFA spectra from the \HI\ archive\footnote{http://arecibo.tc.cornell.edu/hiarchive/alfalfa/default.php} are overplotted. Qualitatively, The VLA spectrum of UGC 12506 closely matches that from ALFALFA. The one for UGC 9037 is less well-matched: the there is significantly more emission in the VLA spectrum compared to the ALFALFA spectrum, particularly in the blueshifted horn of the profile. We do not believe that this asymmetry is real and thus originates either from the higher noise characteristics of the VLA spectrum, %(an rms of 4.4 mJy at 7 \kms\ for the VLA spectrum compared to an rms of 2.3 mJy when smoothed to 10 \kms\ for the ALFALFA data)
 or from the uncertainties in the overall flux scale in the VLA observations.%, or from some extended emission captured in the single-dish ALFALFA observations but not in the interferometric VLA observations.

